course: biology
lesson: 326
title: A puppy at home: the first weeks
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A puppy's first weeks in a new home are like a baby's first weeks at nursery: everything is new, a bit scary and very exciting. It has left its mother, brothers and sisters and the warm pile where it slept.
explain: Picture the first night: the puppy cries in the dark kitchen because it misses that warm pile. A cosy bed nearby and a calm voice help it settle over a few nights.
explain: A puppy chews, nips and wees on the carpet not to be naughty but because it is a baby: its teeth are growing, it explores with its mouth and its bladder is tiny. Patience, chew toys and trips outside teach it far more than shouting.
explain: The first months are when a puppy learns what is safe, so it meets buses, bikes, children and calm dogs, carried in arms until its vaccinations are done.
fact: A new puppy often cries at night because it misses its mother and littermates, and puppies sleep about 18 hours a day while they grow.
fact: Puppies chew while their teeth grow and to explore; a puppy has 28 baby teeth and a grown dog has 42.
fact: A puppy has a tiny bladder, so taking it outside after waking, eating and playing, and praising it there, teaches it quickly; telling it off for accidents makes it hide to wee.
fact: Puppies need their vaccinations before walking where other dogs go, because serious illnesses spread through dog poo and spit; until then a puppy can be carried outside.
fact: The first few months are when a puppy learns what is safe, and this learning window starts closing at around three months old; puppies that meet many calm new things grow into calmer dogs.
fact: Puppies learn how hard to bite by playing with their littermates; if a person says «ouch» and stops the game, the puppy learns gentle mouths keep the fun going.
fact: A puppy's needle-sharp baby teeth fall out at about four to six months.
fact: Puppies are born deaf and blind, their eyes open at about two weeks, and in Britain it is against the law to sell a puppy younger than eight weeks.
fact: Saying the puppy's name and rewarding a look with a tiny treat teaches the name, and short games of a few minutes work better than long lessons.
fact: Socks, small toys and stones can block a dog's gut, and vets take such things out of dogs' stomachs every year.
fact: Most dogs have 18 toes: four on each paw plus a thumb-like dewclaw high on each front leg that does not touch the ground.
fact: Some breeds, like the Norwegian puffin dog, have six toes on each foot for climbing cliffs.
exercise: Why does a new puppy cry on its first night? | It misses its mother and littermates | It is angry with its new family for taking it home | It wants to go out for a long run in the dark | Puppies always cry at the Moon at night
exercise: About how many hours a day does a puppy sleep? | About 18 | About 4 hours | About 8, like a grown-up | It does not sleep at all
exercise: Why does a puppy chew everything? | Its teeth are growing and it explores with its mouth | It is trying to make the family angry, so they will notice it | It is hungry all the time | It wants to dig a hole to the garden
exercise: How many teeth does a grown dog have? | 42 | 28 | 32 | 18
exercise: When is a good time to take a puppy outside for a wee? | After waking, eating and playing | Only once a day, right before bedtime | Only when it barks three times loudly | Never, puppies should learn alone
exercise: What happens if you tell a puppy off for an accident on the carpet? | It learns to hide to wee, not to stop | It learns to go outside and use the garden instead | It stops weeing for ever | It wees only on the sofa instead
exercise: Why must a puppy wait before walking where other dogs go? | It needs its vaccinations against illnesses spread by poo and spit | Its legs are not yet strong enough to walk on hard pavements and paths | Grass is poisonous to young dogs | It has to learn its name first
exercise: Why do puppies meet buses, bikes, children and calm dogs early? | The first months are when they learn what is safe | To tire them out so they sleep through the night for a week | So they can learn to drive | So they forget their mother
exercise: How do puppies first learn how hard to bite? | By playing with their brothers and sisters | By chewing their owners' shoes and slippers | By watching cartoons | From the vet
exercise: When do a puppy's sharp baby teeth fall out? | At about four to six months | At about two weeks after birth | At about five years, when it is old | They never fall out at all, ever
exercise: When do a puppy's eyes open? | At about two weeks old | At birth | At about six months | At about one year
exercise: In Britain, what is the youngest a puppy can be sold at? | Eight weeks | Two days old | One year old | Two weeks old
exercise: How do you teach a puppy its name? | Say the name and reward a look with a tiny treat | Shout it loudly over and over again for a whole hour | Write it on its bowl | Say it once and never again
exercise: Why should socks be picked up before a puppy arrives? | A swallowed sock can block a dog's gut | Socks are far too smelly for a puppy's nose | Puppies are scared stiff of stripy socks | The smell of socks stops puppies sleeping
exercise: How many toes do most dogs have in all? | 18 | 16 | 20 | 24
exercise: Which dog has six toes on each foot for climbing cliffs? | The Norwegian puffin dog | The husky | The poodle | The Labrador
test: «puppy keeps biting me, it's evil» What should he do? | Say «ouch» and stop the game, so it learns gentle mouths | Let it bite harder and harder, so it grows up tough and brave | Shout at it and smack it | Give it away, it will never stop
test: Count them: 101 Dalmatians has 99 puppies plus Pongo and Perdita. How many tails and legs? | 101 tails and 404 legs | 99 tails and 396 legs | 101 tails and 202 legs | 202 tails and 404 legs
test: Count them: 3 puppies sit on a rug. How many toes in all, if each has the usual dewclaws on the front legs? | 54 | 48 | 60 | 36
test: In Tom and Jerry the bulldog Spike walks his puppy Tyke in the park. A real new puppy before its vaccines should be… | Carried in arms, not walked where other dogs go | Walked every day in the busiest dog park in town | Left at home until it is two | Taken swimming with other dogs
test: «it's day one, let's take him to the dog park!!» What is the best reply? | Wait for his vaccinations; carry him outside till then | Great, the more dogs he meets on day one the stronger he gets | Fine if he wears a jumper | Only if the park is muddy
test: «mum shouted at the pup for weeing on the rug, now he wees behind the sofa» Why? | Telling off taught him to hide to wee, not to stop | He likes the sofa more now, because it smells of the family | He cannot see the rug any more | Puppies always wee behind sofas
test: What would happen if a puppy never met new people or traffic until it was a year old? | It might grow up scared of them | It would be calmer than other dogs | It would learn to drive a car instead | Nothing, it would learn later just the same
test: «why's a puppy need more teeth when it grows up» What is the best answer? | A bigger jaw has room for 42 grown-up teeth to eat bigger food | It does not, both have 28 | Old dogs need teeth to talk | Grown dogs eat only soft food
test: «the pup swallowed a sock but he looks fine lol» What is right? | Tell an adult and call the vet, because a sock can block the gut | Feed him another sock to push the first one through, then wait | Leave it, socks melt inside | Give him water and forget it
test: Why are short lessons better than long ones for a puppy? | Puppies forget quickly when they are tired or bored | Long lessons make puppies grow too fast and hurt their legs | Puppies hate treats | Puppies only learn at night
test: «what's a dewclaw even for» What is true about it? | It is a thumb-like toe high on the front leg that does not touch the ground | It is a toe that only puppies have, and it drops off when they are a year old | It is a claw on the dog's tail | It is a tooth that grows on the paw
exam: Identify the reason for which a newly acquired puppy commonly cries during the first night. | It is separated from its mother and littermates | It is displeased with the household | It is unable to see in darkness | It requires exercise at that hour
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the vaccination of a puppy. | Walking in places frequented by other dogs ought to be deferred until vaccination is complete | Vaccination is unnecessary for any animal kept wholly indoors throughout its life | Vaccination is required only after the age of five years | Vaccination is administered solely to prevent fleas
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the dentition of the domestic dog? | The puppy possesses twenty-eight deciduous teeth and the adult forty-two | The puppy possesses forty-two teeth and the adult twenty-eight | Both possess thirty-two teeth | The adult possesses no teeth whatever
exam: Ascertain the consequence of reprimanding a puppy for urinating within the house. | The puppy learns to conceal its urination rather than to cease | The puppy learns promptly to urinate out of doors and never again within the house | The puppy learns to use the lavatory | The puppy ceases to urinate permanently
